<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rio de Janeiro</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rio de Janeiro is a major port city on the south-eastern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. It became the seat of the Portuguese court in 1808 and then served as the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire</a> throughout its existence. Revenues from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> trade financed the modernisation of the city in the nineteenth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro.jpg" alt="Rio de Janeiro" width="320" height="171" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rafael Rabello de Barros · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cidade_Maravilhosa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By mid-century Brazil was producing around half of the world's coffee, and the proceeds financed the modernisation of <b>Rio de Janeiro</b>, the first railways, beginning with the Mauá line of 1854, and the military effort that sustained the Paraguayan War.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Mem de Sá (1557–1573) defeated the Indigenous peoples around Salvador and, with the Jesuits, expelled the French from France Antarctique; his nephew Estácio de Sá founded <b>Rio de Janeiro</b> in 1565.</blockquote><a href="/empire/colonial-brazil">Colonial Brazil · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Between 1808 and 1821 <b>Rio de Janeiro</b> was the seat of the Portuguese monarchy, the only time a European kingdom was ruled from one of its colonies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>International conferences followed in Stockholm in 1972 and in <b>Rio de Janeiro</b> in 1992.</blockquote><a href="/movement/environmental-movement">Environmental movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Days earlier, on 7 April, Brazil's first emperor, Pedro I, had abdicated, leaving his five-year-old son as Emperor Pedro II, and on 13 April the ship carrying him left the port of <b>Rio de Janeiro</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/brazilian-national-anthem">Brazilian National Anthem</a></li><li><blockquote>Built in under four years to plans by Lúcio Costa and Oscar Niemeyer, Brasília replaced <b>Rio de Janeiro</b> as capital.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-21">21 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
